// NEGATIVE TEST -- `bool as char` is refused (N4-04), the fourth cell of the
// matrix and the last direction left unpinned.
//
// MEASURED before the restriction: `true as char` compiled and produced 1 --
// U+0001, START OF HEADING, which is not what anyone writing it meant. The
// conversion had no meaning to give it: `bool` reaches `i64` as 0 or 1 because
// C's `int` is what a Palladium `bool` already is, but nothing makes the first
// control character the character form of "true".
//
// The three siblings cover the other directions: char->bool
// (char_does_not_cast_to_bool.pd), float->char
// (float_does_not_cast_to_char.pd), and the legal char<->i64 pair round-trips
// in tests/02_types_chars.pd. Between them the whole matrix is stated rather
// than assumed, which the ORIGINAL defect needed: the check was a predicate
// over each side and therefore symmetric, so one fixture could never have
// caught a half-repair.
fn main() {
    let c: char = true as char;
    print_int(c as i64);
}
